thinking:
Good.

### Refactoring the paint

Current `helm_dial_elev_paint(bool nav, uint8_t start, uint8_t wn)` computes y and zones. We'll change to use precomputed display data:
- `static int16_t s_dial_smooth[HELM_CLIMB_PROF_N];`
- `static uint8_t s_dial_run_zone[HELM_CLIMB_PROF_N];`
- `static uint8_t s_dial_run_start[HELM_CLIMB_PROF_N];`
- `static uint8_t s_dial_run_len[HELM_CLIMB_PROF_N];`
- `static uint8_t s_dial_run_n;`
- `static int s_dial_wn;` maybe.

Function `helm_dial_profile_build(uint8_t wn, uint32_t step_cm)`:
1. Smooth from `s_climb_eles[start]` — it needs start; pass start.
2. Zone raw per segment (wn-1).
3. Hysteresis into `s_dial_run_zone[]` per segment.
4. Build runs and merge short.
5. Store run arrays.

Then paint uses `s_dial_smooth`, `s_dial_run_zone`/runs, and `s_dial_wn`.

Colors:
```
static uint32_t helm_dial_zone_fill(uint8_t z) {
  if (z == 2) return HELM_COLOR_ELEV_STEEP;
  if (z == 1) return HELM_COLOR_ELEV_FLAT;
  return HELM_COLOR_ELEV_DONE;
}
static uint32_t helm_dial_zone_ink(uint8_t z) {
  if (z == 2) return HELM_COLOR_ELEV_STEEP_INK;
  if (z == 1) return HELM_COLOR_ELEV_FLAT_INK;
  return HELM_COLOR_ELEV_DONE_INK;
}
```
S9's mapping: flat → gray (ELEV_DONE), mid → green (ELEV_FLAT), steep → red (ELEV_STEEP). Yes.

Fill opacity: `LV_OPA_40` (S9's `HELM_CLIMB_FILL_OPA`). The user says 浅填充. Use `LV_OPA_40` (102/255). On the canvas, drawing a rect with alpha over paper gives a pale tint. The ridge 2px opaque dark.

For the fill column scan, use `lv_draw_rect_dsc_t` with `bg_opa = LV_OPA_40`, color = fill hex via `helm_color_snap` or `helm_color`? S9 used `helm_color`? For alpha blending, use the quantized color; `helm_color_snap` returns a `lv_color_t`. Use `helm_color_snap(fill)`.

For the ridge, one polyline per run:
```
for each run r:
   int first_seg = run.start, last_seg = run.start+run.len-1;
   int pcount = run.len + 1;
   for k=0..run.len: x = x_of(first_seg+k); y = y_of(first_seg+k); pts[k]=(x,y)
   ld.points = pts; ld.point_cnt = pcount; ...
   lv_draw_line(&layer,&ld);
```
Note: `x_of(i)` maps segment index i (0..wn-1) to x = i*(W-1)/(wn-1). `y_of(i)` from smoothed value.
- The line width 2, round start/end.
- Runs cover the full segment range; consecutive runs share endpoints, so the polyline is continuous with color changes at run boundaries.
- The color per run = zone ink of run.zone.

Fill column scan:
- For each x=0..W-1, find `num = x*(wn-1)`, seg = num/(W-1), rem = num%(W-1), y = interpolate smooth[seg], smooth[seg+1].
- The zone for column = `run_zone[seg]` (per-segment post-merge array). Fill color = zone fill.
- Draw rect x..x, y..base with 40% opacity. Adjacent columns don't overlap.
- At x=W-1, seg = wn-1 → clamp to wn-2 and y = smooth[wn-1].
- Fill color changes at segment boundaries, but with hysteresis/merge, long runs; no barcode.

Smoothing:
```
static void helm_dial_smooth(const int16_t *src, int16_t *dst, uint8_t n)
{
  for i:
    int lo = i-2; if (lo<0) lo=0;
    int hi = i+2; if (hi>=n) hi=n-1;
    int sum=0; for k=lo..hi sum+=src[k];
    dst[i] = (sum + (hi-lo+1)/2) / (hi-lo+1);
}
```
Use rounding. Note src is `s_climb_eles+start`; dst local static.

Hysteresis:
```
uint8_t cur = raw[0];
for i=0..wn-2:
   if (i==0) { hz[0]=cur; continue; }
   if (raw[i] != cur && raw[i] == raw[i-1]) cur = raw[i];
   hz[i] = cur;
```
This implements 2 consecutive raw samples confirming the new zone. If raw changes for one segment, it's ignored. Good.

Runs build:
```
int nr=0; i=0;
while (i<wn-1) {
  int j=i+1;
  while (j<wn-1 && hz[j]==hz[i]) j++;
  run_start[nr]=i; run_len[nr]=j-i; run_zone[nr]=hz[i]; nr++; i=j;
}
```
Merge short runs:
```
while (nr > 1) {
  int idx=-1;
  for k=0..nr-1 if (run_len[k] < 3) { idx=k; break; }
  if (idx<0) break;
  if (idx==0) {
     // merge run0 into run1 (zone=run1.zone)
     run_start[1] = run_start[0];
     run_len[1] += run_len[0];
     // remove run0
     for k=1..nr-1: run[k-1]=run[k]; nr--;
  } else {
     // merge idx into idx-1
     run_len[idx-1] += run_len[idx];
     for k=idx+1..nr-1: run[k-1]=run[k]; nr--;
  }
}
```
But after merging idx into prev, the prev zone stays; the merged run length grows. Fine. If idx==0 (first run short), merge into next; the next zone wins. Good.
- Edge case: nr becomes 1; all segments one zone. Fine.
- The array `s_dial_run_zone` per segment after merging: fill zone per segment should be rebuilt from runs:
```
for each run r: for k=0..len-1: seg_zone[start+k]=zone;
```
Use `s_dial_seg_zone[60]`.

Zone raw computation:
```
static uint8_t helm_dial_raw_zone(int16_t e0, int16_t e1, uint32_t step_cm)
{
  uint32_t de, g;
  if (step_cm < 1) return 0; // flat / no distance
  de = abs...
  g = de*10000/step_cm;
  if (g < HELM_CLIMB_GRADE_FLAT) return 0;
  if (g < HELM_CLIMB_GRADE_STEEP) return 1;
  return 2;
}
```
This mirrors `helm_climb_grade_zone`. We can reuse `helm_climb_grade_zone(e0,e1,step_cm)` directly! It returns 0/1/2 with the same thresholds. It's a static function defined earlier. Use it. But S9's mapping uses the same thresholds (HELM_CLIMB_GRADE_FLAT=1, STEEP=4). Good.

